# Line endings are normalised so a checksum means the same thing on every platform.
#
# Without this, Git with core.autocrlf=true gives a Windows worktree CRLF while the
# committed blob stays LF. Any pin taken from the worktree then fails on Linux, even
# though the content is identical. That is exactly what happened to ten governance
# checksums and to 118 rows of the E2-R1 release manifest; configs/line_ending_provenance.yaml
# and docs/e2r1_release_manifest_errata.json record it.
#
# Safe to apply here: no tracked text blob at v1.0.0 contains a CR byte, so
# normalisation changes no committed content. The binary types below are listed
# explicitly rather than left to heuristics -- a Parquet file or a workbook that got
# line-ending conversion would be corrupted, not merely reformatted.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.png       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.xls       binary
*.xlsx      binary
*.parquet   binary
*.whl       binary
*.gz        binary
*.pkl       binary
*.joblib    binary
